{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q06",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "Why does deduplication matter differently for fine-tuning data versus a retrieval corpus?",
  "answer": "Deduplication has two distinct impacts depending on where the data lives:\n\n* **Fine\u2011tuning data** \u2013 When the same example appears many times, the model sees that example with a higher effective weight than intended.\u202fThis over\u2011represents certain phrases or facts, can bias the fine\u2011tuned model toward those patterns, and can even cause the model to memorise and later regurgitate the duplicated passages verbatim instead of learning to generalise\u202f[deduplication-in-training-and-corpus-data::0].\n\n* **Retrieval corpus** \u2013 In a corpus, near\u2011duplicate notes compete for the same top\u2011k retrieval slots.\u202fBecause several duplicates occupy the same slot, they crowd out other distinct, relevant chunks, lowering recall@k and giving the model repeated evidence rather than a variety of evidence\u202f[deduplication-in-training-and-corpus-data::0].\n\nThus, for fine\u2011tuning duplicates distort training dynamics, while for retrieval they waste space and hurt the ability to surface diverse, relevant evidence.",
